Here's a C++ program that reads a text file line by line and counts the number of occurrences of a specific word in the file:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

int countWordOccurences(std::ifstream& inputFile, std::string wordToCount) {
    int wordCount = 0;

    // Read the file line by line
    std::string line;
    while (std::getline(inputFile, line)) {
        // Make the line string lowercase for case-insensitive comparison
        std::transform(line.begin(), line.end(), line.begin(), ::tolower);
        // Find all occurrences of the word in the current line
        size_t pos = line.find(wordToCount);
        while (pos != std::string::npos) {
            wordCount++;
            pos = line.find(wordToCount, pos + wordToCount.length());
        }
    }
    return wordCount;
}

int main() {
    std::string wordToCount = "specific";  // The word to count in the file
    std::string fileName = "text_file.txt";  // The name of the text file to read

    std::ifstream inputFile(fileName);
    if (!inputFile.is_open()) {
        std::cerr << "Error opening file " << fileName << std::endl;
        return -1;
    }

    int wordCount = countWordOccurences(inputFile, wordToCount);
    std::cout << "The word '" << wordToCount << "' appears " << wordCount
              << " time(s) in the file." << std::endl;

    inputFile.close();
    return 0;
}
```

This program takes two arguments: the name of the text file to read and the word to count. The `countWordOccurences` function reads and processes each line of the file, checking for occurrences of the specified word (`wordToCount`). By default, it performs a case-insensitive search, so the word found can be either uppercase or lowercase. The program opens the file, computes the word count, and displays the result. It closes the file and returns 0 to indicate successful execution.
